{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"thorough-turophilic-airedale","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# A rejected tickets-branch auto-push fails SILENTLY, and ticket event payloads are never scanned for secrets\n\nTwo hardening items carried forward from [rebar:94d0-5d19-2d06-4fa7], which is closed: its\nincident (live credentials in a ticket comment) is resolved — credentials rotated, history\nscrubbed, branch pushing again — but the two conditions that let it run for ~8 hours undetected\nare properties of the store, not of that incident, and remain.\n\n## 1. A rejected auto-push is invisible to the agent that wrote\n\n**This is the more dangerous of the two.** rebar's store auto-commits and auto-pushes every\nwrite to `sync.remote`. When that push is REJECTED, the write still succeeds locally and the\ncommand reports success. Nothing surfaces.\n\nMeasured during the incident: **122 ticket commits** accumulated locally over roughly eight\nhours while every push was rejected by GitHub push protection. Every `rebar comment`,\n`rebar transition` and `rebar create` in that window returned normally. The divergence was\ndiscovered only because a completely unrelated CI failure (`require resolvable rebar ticket` on\nGerrit change 1343) prompted a manual `git push`, which finally printed the rejection.\n\nConsequences observed, not hypothesised:\n * a Gerrit change could not pass its ticket gate, because the ticket it referenced existed only\n   locally — and the error pointed at the change, not at the store;\n * evidence recorded on epic children was invisible to CI and to every other agent session,\n   while appearing perfectly normal to the session that wrote it.\n\nThe store's whole premise is \"every write is shared immediately\" (`docs/concurrency.md`). A push\nthat fails silently breaks that premise while preserving its appearance, which is strictly worse\nthan failing loudly.\n\n**Shape of a fix (not prescribing):** surface the rejection to the writing caller — a warning on\nthe command that triggered it, a non-zero signal, or a visible \"N commits unpushed\" state that\nsubsequent commands report. What must not persist is a rejection that no one sees.\n\n## 2. Ticket event payloads are never scanned for secrets\n\nrebar runs `gitleaks` as its code-review secret gate, but that gate sees CODE. A ticket COMMENT\nis the one place an agent routinely pastes raw environment output, provider configuration, or a\nfailing command's stderr — exactly the material that carries credentials. Nothing scans it on\nwrite.\n\nIn the incident, an Anthropic API key and a GitHub PAT reached a committed comment payload. They\nnever reached GitHub, but only because GitHub's own push protection caught them at the boundary\n— a control rebar does not own and cannot rely on for a store that may one day push somewhere\nwithout it.\n\n**Shape of a fix (not prescribing):** scan event payloads on write, or at minimum on the\ncomment/description paths. Worth weighing against write latency and false positives — a noisy\ngate on every comment would be its own problem, which is why this is a decision to record rather\nthan an obvious change to make.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a rejected auto-push is surfaced to the writing caller rather than failing silently\n- [ ] a test drives a REJECTED push and asserts the caller sees it (not a mock's call count)\n- [ ] a recorded decision on scanning ticket event payloads for secrets on write — including an\n      explicit \"no, and here is why\" if that is the answer","id":"2a76-c9d9-4e8a-471b","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"A rejected tickets-branch auto-push fails silently; ticket event payloads are never secret-scanned"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785860292482390001,"uuid":"27c6efbb-9a8f-412c-8895-acac6d06c52c"}